  nodeAbstract: 
  nodeTextLayers:
  - title: What is membrane fusion?
    basic: "An example of membrane fusion can be seen here. \nIn general, membrane
      fusion is an interaction where two lipid membranes merge together, either partially
      or fully, into a single continuous membrane."
    intermediate: 
    advanced: 
  - title: How do the membranes fuse?
    basic: "The SNARE complex brings two lipid membranes into close proximity. \nThe
      lipid molecules rearrange into one continuous membrane through hydrophobic
      interactions."
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "SNARE proteins anchored on both the vesicle and cell membrane intertwine
      into a tight bundle through a process known as zipping. \nThis interaction
      overcomes the repulsive force between the membranes, causing them to fuse. "
  - title: Why is membrane fusion important?
    basic: Here, fusion of plasma and vesicle membranes creates a channel for insulin
      to leave the cell and enter the bloodstream.
    intermediate: 
    advanced: 
  nodeSpriteLayer: []
